{"name":"Cricket Field","hex":"#c3d29c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cricket field","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The area of grass (within the boundary) on which a cricket match is played.","A cricket ground."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cricket_field"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cricket field","blocks":[{"text":"A cricket field or cricket ground or cricket oval is a large grass field on which the game of cricket is played. Although generally oval in shape, there is a wide variety within this: perfect circles, elongated ovals, rounded rectangles, or irregular shapes with little or no symmetry – but they will have smooth boundaries without sharp corners, almost without exception. There are no fixed dimensions for the field but its diameter usually varies between 450 and 500 feet for men's cricket, and between 360 feet (110 m) and 420 feet (130 m) for women's cricket."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Cricket_field_parts.svg/330px-Cricket_field_parts.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cricket_field","wikidata":"Q682943"},{"title":"Cricket","blocks":[{"text":"Cricket is a bat-and-ball game that is played between two teams of eleven players on a field, at the centre of which is a 22-yard pitch with a wicket at each end, each comprising two bails balanced on three stumps. Two players from the batting team, the striker and nonstriker, stand in front of either wicket holding bats, while one player from the fielding team, the bowler, bowls the ball toward the striker's wicket from the opposite end of the pitch. The striker's goal is to hit the bowled ball with the bat and then switch places with the nonstriker, with the batting team scoring one run for each of these swaps. Runs are also scored when the ball reaches the boundary of the field or when the ball is bowled illegally."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Pollock_to_Hussey.jpg/330px-Pollock_to_Hussey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cricket","wikidata":"Q5375"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg/250px-Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg/250px-Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A medieval \"club ball\" game involving an underarm bowl towards a batter. Ball catchers are shown positioning themselves to catch a ball. Detail from the Canticles of Holy Mary, 13th century.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Historical_cricket_bat_art.jpg/500px-Historical_cricket_bat_art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Historical_cricket_bat_art.jpg/500px-Historical_cricket_bat_art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Evolution of the cricket bat. The original \"hockey stick\" (left) evolved into the straight bat from c. 1760, when pitched delivery bowling began.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHistorical_cricket_bat_art.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Francis_Cotes_-_The_young_cricketer_%281768%29.jpg/500px-Francis_Cotes_-